## Preliminaries: check your setup

Evaluate this cell after setup finishes. A **satisfiable** result showing `ready`
confirms that the bundled clingo is running inside the notebook. Each solve
closes its own native control; this check is safe to reevaluate.

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("../support/course.exs", __DIR__))
alias ASPCourse, as: Course
alias Clingo.Control

Clingo.solve!("ready. #show ready/0.") |> Course.display()
```

## Facts, rules and shown answers

### What you will learn

Read a rule, derive an answer and control what the notebook displays.

### Build one complete program

A fact supplies information; a rule derives an atom when every body condition holds. Uppercase names are variables. This example classifies fictional beacons: Cedar is awake, has a small reach and calls. Each solve starts with its own program.

```elixir
# Facts and rules are ordinary ASP, inside an Elixir string.
result =
  Clingo.solve!(
    ~S"""
    awake(cedar).
    awake(birch).
    awake(maple).
    reach(cedar, "small").
    reach(birch, "large").
    reach(elm, "small").
    reach(maple, "small").
    calls(cedar).
    calls(elm).
    beacon(Creature) :- awake(Creature), reach(Creature, "small"), calls(Creature).
    #show beacon/1.
    """,
    models: :all
  )

[%{shown: ["beacon(cedar)"]}] = result.models
Course.display(result)
```

### Separate models from display

The rule still derives its answer when a show directive hides every atom. An empty shown list is one model, whereas an inconsistent program has no models. These are different outcomes.

```elixir
hidden = Clingo.solve!("signal. #show.", models: :all)
[%{shown: []}] = hidden.models
:satisfiable = hidden.status
impossible = Clingo.solve!("signal. :- signal.")
[] = impossible.models
:unsatisfiable = impossible.status
%{hidden: hidden.status, impossible: impossible.status}
```

### Try it yourself

Before running it, predict the effect of adding awake(elm). to the first program. Then make that one edit.

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

Elm already has the other two properties. The shown model should now contain beacon(cedar) and beacon(elm).

</details>

## Classify creatures with rules

Run top to bottom. Edit the ASP inside the string, then reevaluate the cell and any dependent cells. Solves close their controls automatically. [Wrapper guide](../elixir/01-getting-started.livemd#getting-started-with-clingo_elixir) · [Lesson contents](../README.md).

We will classify imaginary creatures from three properties: being alive, having short arms, and roaring. The names are Riley, Morgan, Casey and Jordan. These are symbolic constants, not variables. A fact such as `alive(riley).` states one piece of information; its final period is required.

Each code cell is a separate program. The first lists living creatures. The next lists attributes without carrying facts forward from the previous solve. The combined example supplies both kinds of information before applying a rule.

```elixir
~S"""
alive(riley).
alive(morgan).
alive(casey).
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

### Add attributes

The first solve contains `alive/1` facts. A predicate’s arity is its number of arguments. Now introduce `arm_length/2` and `roars/1`. Missing facts do not make a program invalid; they simply provide no support for the corresponding positive atom.

```elixir
~S"""
arm_length(riley, "small").
arm_length(morgan, "large").
arm_length(jordan, "small").
arm_length(casey, "small").
roars(riley).
roars(jordan).
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

### Combine conditions

Read `resembles_dinosaur(Creature) :- alive(Creature), arm_length(Creature,"small"), roars(Creature).` as an implication. The body requires all three conditions for the same creature. Uppercase names denote variables; lowercase names denote symbolic constants. The next cell includes the complete input, so Riley satisfies the rule.

```elixir
~S"""
alive(riley).
alive(morgan).
alive(casey).
arm_length(riley, "small").
arm_length(morgan, "large").
arm_length(jordan, "small").
arm_length(casey, "small").
roars(riley).
roars(jordan).
resembles_dinosaur(Creature) :- alive(Creature), arm_length(Creature, "small"), roars(Creature).
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

### Choose what to display

The full model includes facts as well as derived atoms. `#show resembles_dinosaur/1.` restricts the shown output without changing which models solve the program.

```elixir
~S"""
alive(riley).
alive(morgan).
alive(casey).
arm_length(riley, "small").
arm_length(morgan, "large").
arm_length(jordan, "small").
arm_length(casey, "small").
roars(riley).
roars(jordan).
resembles_dinosaur(Creature) :- alive(Creature), arm_length(Creature, "small"), roars(Creature).
#show resembles_dinosaur/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

The filtered result contains exactly `resembles_dinosaur(riley)`. Casey lacks a roar, Jordan lacks an `alive/1` fact, and Morgan has large arms.

Try giving Jordan an `alive/1` fact, then rerun the complete program. Two creatures should now qualify. Removing all `alive/1` facts instead removes support for the classification; it does not necessarily cause a parsing error. Use the blank cell for further experiments.

## Express alternatives without body disjunction

Run top to bottom. Edit the ASP inside the string, then reevaluate the cell and any dependent cells. Solves close their controls automatically. [Wrapper guide](../elixir/01-getting-started.livemd#getting-started-with-clingo_elixir) · [Lesson contents](../README.md).

A rule body joined by commas requires every condition. To derive `four_sided/1` from alternative shape names, use several rules sharing the same head. Putting a head-style `|` disjunction directly into the body is not valid syntax: the first cell deliberately demonstrates that parsing error.

> **Note.** The body-OR example is deliberately invalid. Its native parsing diagnostic is displayed as an expected error so later examples can evaluate.

```elixir
Course.expect_error(fn ->
  ~S"""
  figure("circle").
  figure("triangle").
  figure("rectangle").
  figure("rhombus").
  figure("square").
  figure("parallelogram").
  figure("trapezium").
  figure("kite").
  four_sided(Name)
        :- figure(Name),
          Name == "rectangle" | Name == "square" | Name == "rhombus" | Name == "trapezium" | Name == "parallogram".
  """
  |> Clingo.solve!(models: :all)
  |> Course.display()
end)
```

### Establish one case

The next program recognizes the rectangle alone. This is a working rule, but it does not yet cover every four-sided shape in the facts.

```elixir
~S"""
figure("circle").
figure("triangle").
figure("rectangle").
figure("rhombus").
figure("square").
figure("parallelogram").
figure("trapezium").
figure("kite").
four_sided(Name)
      :- figure(Name),
        Name == "rectangle".
#show four_sided/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

### Cover each alternative

Give each accepted shape its own rule. Any one of those rules can support the corresponding `four_sided/1` atom. The input also includes a circle and a triangle; neither belongs in this classification.

```elixir
~S"""
figure("circle").
figure("triangle").
figure("rectangle").
figure("rhombus").
figure("square").
figure("parallelogram").
figure("trapezium").
figure("kite").
four_sided(Name)
      :- figure(Name),
        Name == "rectangle".
four_sided(Name)
      :- figure(Name),
        Name == "square".
four_sided(Name)
      :- figure(Name),
        Name == "rhombus".
four_sided(Name)
      :- figure(Name),
        Name == "parallelogram".
four_sided(Name)
      :- figure(Name),
        Name == "trapezium".
four_sided(Name)
      :- figure(Name),
        Name == "kite".
#show four_sided/1.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

The final model shows six four-sided shapes: rectangle, square, rhombus, parallelogram, trapezium and kite. Circle and triangle are absent from the shown classification.

As an exercise, represent the accepted names as data and use a single classification rule. Check that the set of six shown atoms stays the same.

## Derive positive consequences

Start from facts and repeatedly apply positive rules. For these positive normal programs, stable-model semantics selects the least supported consequence set; unsupported cycles do not create facts.

The programs below are independent variations. Predict each answer set before running the cell. Edit one program in the list and compare its row again; answer-set order has no meaning.

```elixir
programs = [
  ~S"""
  a.
  b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a.
  b.
  c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a.
  b :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a.
  b :- a.
  c :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  b :- a.
  a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  b :- a.
  a.
  c :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  c :- a, b.
  a.
  b :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  c :- a, b.
  a.
  b :- a.
  d :- a, b, c.
  """,
  ~S"""
  b :- a.
  """,
  ~S"""
  b :- a.
  c :- b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a.
  c :- b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a.
  c :- b.
  e :- d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a.
  c :- a, b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  a.
  c :- a, b.
  e :- a, d.
  """,
  ~S"""
  d :- a.
  a.
  c :- b.
  """,
  ~S"""
  d :- a.
  a.
  c :- b.
  e :- a, d.
  """
]

programs
|> Enum.with_index(1)
|> Enum.map(fn {program, index} ->
  result = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)

  %{
    example: index,
    program: program,
    shown_sets: inspect(Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))),
    status: result.status,
    exhausted: result.exhausted
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```
